Explanation: Erlotinib is a tyrosine kinase inhibitor (TKI) that targets the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR). In non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), the presence of an activating mutation in the EGFR gene (often an exon 19 deletion or L858R substitution) is a common driver. These mutations lead to constitutive activation of the EGFR, which normally signals through the receptor to promote cell proliferation. By inhibiting the EGFR, erlotinib blocks this proliferative signal, causing the cancer cells to arrest in the G1 phase of the cell cycle, preventing uncontrolled division.
